course: dictionary
lesson: 3010
title: One word, many meanings: duty
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word duty has more than one meaning. Look at the words around it to find out which one the writer means. «Every person has rights, such as the right to be safe, and duties, such as respecting others and following the rules.» Here a duty is something you must do because it is right or because the rules say so.
explain: The same word can mean a job you are given. A teacher on playground duty is watching the children at break time. The clues are the playground and the children, so duty means work that is part of a job. Always ask: what word would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Duty also appears in phrases. «On duty» means working right now, and «off duty» means not working. A nurse who is on duty is at work, while a nurse who is off duty is at home. Phrases like «heavy-duty» and «duty-free» have their own meanings, so learn them as chunks.
fact: Duty means something you must do because it is right, because it is your job, or because the rules or the law say so.
fact: «On duty» means working right now; «off duty» means not working.
fact: «Duty-free» means no tax is paid on the goods, and «heavy-duty» means very strong.
fact: A duty can be a tax, especially on goods brought in from another country.
fact: Rights are things you are free to have, such as the right to learn, and duties are things you must do, such as following the rules.
exercise: «Every person has rights, such as the right to be safe, and duties, such as respecting others and following the rules.» Which one is a duty? | To be safe | To be treated fairly | *Respecting others | To learn
exercise: A teacher is on playground duty at break time. What does duty mean here? | A tax paid on goods from another country | *Work that is part of a job, watching the children | A bell that rings at the end of the day | A game played in the yard
exercise: What does «on duty» mean? | *Working right now | Going on holiday | Lost in the playground | Resting at home
test: Which phrase means that no tax is paid on the goods? | Off duty | Heavy-duty | *Duty-free | On duty
test: «Rules matter, but sometimes a more important duty, like fetching help for a hurt child, outweighs a small rule.» Which is more important in this sentence? | A small rule | Both are exactly the same | Neither of them matters | *Fetching help for a hurt child
exam: «The knight did his duty.» What does this mean? | He went on holiday to the seaside | *He did what was right and expected of him | He ate his dinner in the castle | He hid from the battle
